1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:29,980
We'll be right back.

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:46,240
Well, good morning, church.

3
00:00:46,240 --> 00:00:48,960
Morning, Gary.

4
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:53,240
So good to see all of you.

5
00:00:53,240 --> 00:00:58,960
I hope you had a happy new year and you enjoyed the Advent and Christmas season and you were

6
00:00:58,960 --> 00:01:03,840
able, hopefully, to get some margin, to spend some time with God and others, loved ones,

7
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:04,320
family.

8
00:01:04,320 --> 00:01:09,280
For many of us, oh, did my kids' badge get called?

9
00:01:09,280 --> 00:01:10,440
That's like the first time ever.

10
00:01:10,440 --> 00:01:11,900
I've always been waiting for this opportunity.

11
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:13,280
Yes.

12
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:17,800
There's forgiveness in Jesus, even for the pastor's child.

13
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:18,160
All right.

14
00:01:18,160 --> 00:01:18,700
That's awesome.

15
00:01:19,380 --> 00:01:21,120
I always wondered if it was going to happen.

16
00:01:21,120 --> 00:01:21,760
Now it happened.

17
00:01:21,760 --> 00:01:22,320
This is great.

18
00:01:22,320 --> 00:01:22,880
All right.

19
00:01:22,880 --> 00:01:25,720
Potty training will do that, probably.

20
00:01:25,720 --> 00:01:26,740
That's awesome.

21
00:01:26,740 --> 00:01:29,680
Everyone, bless Marissa as she goes and deals with that.

22
00:01:29,680 --> 00:01:33,800
For many of us, this is just another week.

23
00:01:33,800 --> 00:01:36,280
How many of us are like, New Year's doesn't mean anything to me.

24
00:01:36,280 --> 00:01:38,800
It's just another week on the calendar.

25
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:39,140
All right.

26
00:01:39,140 --> 00:01:39,660
That's fair.

27
00:01:40,220 --> 00:01:45,100
For others of us, it feels like this opportunity to take an inventory of the past year, maybe

28
00:01:45,100 --> 00:01:50,700
hit a reset button on a few things and kind of reorient the way that we've been doing life

29
00:01:50,700 --> 00:01:51,800
for the past year.

30
00:01:51,800 --> 00:01:57,660
Whether you're a big New Year's person or not, I would argue that every Sunday morning

31
00:01:57,660 --> 00:02:00,420
is an opportunity for us to have a fresh start.

32
00:02:00,420 --> 00:02:05,880
It's time for us to come to the table of the Lord with our brothers and sisters in Christ

33
00:02:05,880 --> 00:02:08,000
and be reminded of what matters most.

34
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:14,900
It's time for us to receive mercy and forgiveness from Jesus for the way the worries and the

35
00:02:14,900 --> 00:02:20,660
pleasures of this world have distracted us, for the ways that we have oriented our life

36
00:02:20,660 --> 00:02:22,200
towards other things in the gospel.

37
00:02:22,200 --> 00:02:26,140
Every Sunday morning is an opportunity for us to recenter ourselves on God.

38
00:02:27,180 --> 00:02:32,360
And as a church, the first few Sundays of the year is usually an opportunity for me or our

39
00:02:32,360 --> 00:02:33,900
leadership to cast some vision.

40
00:02:33,900 --> 00:02:36,940
It's a time for us to take inventory as an organization.

41
00:02:36,940 --> 00:02:42,740
Where have we been over the last year and where do we believe we are called to go over this

42
00:02:42,740 --> 00:02:43,460
next year?

43
00:02:43,460 --> 00:02:48,160
And so for the next two weeks, we're going to do a little mini series called Radical Life

44
00:02:48,160 --> 00:02:49,060
where I hope to do that.

45
00:02:49,060 --> 00:02:53,860
I hope to kind of take some inventory of where we've been as a church and where I believe and

46
00:02:53,860 --> 00:02:56,460
we believe we are headed as a church in 2026.

47
00:02:56,460 --> 00:02:58,020
Does that sound like a good plan to everybody?

48
00:02:58,020 --> 00:02:59,560
Awesome.

49
00:02:59,560 --> 00:03:00,820
Thank you for the encouragement.

50
00:03:00,820 --> 00:03:01,780
That's wonderful.

51
00:03:01,780 --> 00:03:04,320
Let's pray and we'll do just that.

52
00:03:04,320 --> 00:03:08,080
Father in heaven, holy is your name.

53
00:03:08,080 --> 00:03:15,780
Father, you have done everything.

54
00:03:15,980 --> 00:03:22,580
You have accomplished everything for us in Jesus and you have invited us to partner with him

55
00:03:22,580 --> 00:03:28,060
in obedience and surrender to see your kingdom come and your will be done on earth as it is

56
00:03:28,060 --> 00:03:28,400
in heaven.

57
00:03:28,400 --> 00:03:37,080
God, may we as a church have a singular goal, a singular focus, which is that surrender to

58
00:03:37,080 --> 00:03:38,400
you, obedience to you.

59
00:03:39,760 --> 00:03:41,860
And God, may we leave the results in your hands.

60
00:03:41,860 --> 00:03:47,700
May this not be a thing where we leverage business principles to in our own power and strength

61
00:03:47,700 --> 00:03:48,940
accomplish your purposes.

62
00:03:48,940 --> 00:03:53,740
But may we just follow you in obedience and leave the results to you.

63
00:03:53,740 --> 00:03:59,360
Father, give us clarity and wisdom this morning as we look at your Scriptures.

64
00:03:59,500 --> 00:04:01,480
Holy Spirit, have your way in the room.

65
00:04:01,480 --> 00:04:06,560
Translate whatever comes out of my mouth and to whatever you want each individual to hear.

66
00:04:07,860 --> 00:04:14,540
So that it is not a general message to a hypothetical group of people, but it is a specific message

67
00:04:14,540 --> 00:04:18,160
for the individuals in this room that you have brought here on purpose.

68
00:04:18,160 --> 00:04:19,960
May we hear from you.

69
00:04:19,960 --> 00:04:23,960
In Jesus' name I pray.

70
00:04:23,960 --> 00:04:24,820
Amen.

71
00:04:24,820 --> 00:04:30,800
If you have your Bibles and you want to turn to Acts chapter 16, we're going to be in Acts chapter 16

72
00:04:30,800 --> 00:04:32,900
and a little bit of 15 here in just a minute.

73
00:04:32,900 --> 00:04:35,980
Today's message started a bit weird for me.

74
00:04:36,700 --> 00:04:42,220
I had one verse in my mind that I wanted to share because this one verse, it just kept

75
00:04:42,220 --> 00:04:48,400
coming back into my head because I think it encapsulates what our 2025 focus was as a church

76
00:04:48,400 --> 00:04:55,360
and also what I believe our 2026 focus should be, and it holds them together in tension.

77
00:04:55,360 --> 00:05:00,860
If you were around at the beginning of last year, you might remember me introducing our year

78
00:05:00,860 --> 00:05:02,400
focus on making room.

79
00:05:02,400 --> 00:05:04,680
I talked a lot about this idea of making room.

80
00:05:04,720 --> 00:05:09,820
As a church, we had grown by 40 or 50 people every year for three years, and we just wanted

81
00:05:09,820 --> 00:05:13,600
to make space for people who are outside the church to be able to come in, that make sure

82
00:05:13,600 --> 00:05:17,880
there are chairs and there are parking spots and there are small groups and there are children's

83
00:05:17,880 --> 00:05:22,480
ministry volunteers, and a lot of awesome things happened from that focus.

84
00:05:22,480 --> 00:05:27,360
This is the kind of first part of our mission statement, which is inviting real people with real lives.

85
00:05:27,820 --> 00:05:29,880
We want to be an invitational culture.

86
00:05:29,880 --> 00:05:31,360
We want to be a welcoming culture.

87
00:05:31,360 --> 00:05:36,860
We want people to come in and feel like we made space for them, like they are welcome in our

88
00:05:36,860 --> 00:05:37,880
community.

89
00:05:37,880 --> 00:05:39,500
We made a lot of progress.

90
00:05:39,500 --> 00:05:43,540
More seats, more parking, a better follow-up process, more small groups.

91
00:05:43,540 --> 00:05:44,680
All of these things happened.

92
00:05:44,680 --> 00:05:46,040
It was really great.

93
00:05:46,920 --> 00:05:49,400
And I don't want to lose sight of that focus.

94
00:05:49,400 --> 00:05:53,000
I always want us to be a welcoming and invitational church.

95
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:59,220
However, I think oftentimes when you put an intentional focus on one thing, you can lose

96
00:05:59,220 --> 00:06:02,180
sight of the opposite end of the spectrum.

97
00:06:02,700 --> 00:06:09,140
And oftentimes when churches put an intentional focus on those outside the church, it's easy

98
00:06:09,140 --> 00:06:12,700
for us to take our eyes off of the second part of our mission statement, which is inviting

99
00:06:12,700 --> 00:06:16,200
real people with real lives into a Christ-centered community.

100
00:06:16,200 --> 00:06:18,220
Christ-centered communities don't happen on accident.

101
00:06:18,220 --> 00:06:24,620
Those of us who are inside the church already, we have to be cultivating this Christ-centered

102
00:06:24,620 --> 00:06:25,200
community.

103
00:06:25,200 --> 00:06:26,980
We will not fall into it.

104
00:06:26,980 --> 00:06:30,880
And so if we want to accomplish both things, if we want to be an invitational church that

105
00:06:30,880 --> 00:06:34,620
forms a Christ-centered community, we have to both focus on making room for those outside

106
00:06:34,620 --> 00:06:38,120
the church and maturing the faith of those who are inside the church.

107
00:06:38,120 --> 00:06:39,260
Does that make sense?

108
00:06:39,260 --> 00:06:46,080
So I really feel this desire, this intention to focus on the maturing of the faith of the

109
00:06:46,080 --> 00:06:52,780
believer inside the church, not forsaking being a welcoming church on the outside, but being

110
00:06:52,780 --> 00:06:58,080
intentional to invite those of you who have called this church home to pursue maturity of

111
00:06:58,080 --> 00:07:02,660
faith and being conformed to the image of Jesus so that what we are inviting people into really

112
00:07:02,660 --> 00:07:06,100
is a Christ-centered community.

113
00:07:06,100 --> 00:07:12,180
So I really, like I said, I don't want to forsake the emphasis on making room, but I do want

114
00:07:12,180 --> 00:07:17,120
to shift our focus a little bit this year to maturing faith, which leads me to that first

115
00:07:17,120 --> 00:07:20,940
verse I talked about that's been spinning around in my head for the last month or so.

116
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:26,020
We find it in Acts chapter 16, verse 5, where Luke, the author of Acts, records this.

117
00:07:26,020 --> 00:07:28,080
It's a kind of summary statement here.

118
00:07:28,080 --> 00:07:35,080
He says, so the churches were strengthened in faith and grew daily in numbers.

119
00:07:35,080 --> 00:07:40,580
Do we see both these things happening in tension together in the early church?

120
00:07:40,580 --> 00:07:45,840
They were both maturing and strengthening in faith, and they were growing daily in numbers.

121
00:07:45,840 --> 00:07:50,560
They were in an invitational community where people from the outside were coming in and finding

122
00:07:50,560 --> 00:07:54,500
a welcome space for them to be brought into the family of God.

123
00:07:54,500 --> 00:07:57,740
And also those who were inside the church were being strengthened.

124
00:07:57,740 --> 00:08:03,960
At this point in the history of the early church, the church was effective at both numerical growth

125
00:08:03,960 --> 00:08:13,080
and spiritual maturity, welcoming those outside the church and maturing those inside the church.

126
00:08:13,080 --> 00:08:18,660
And if you're in here and you've been a part of many churches, you grew up in the church

127
00:08:18,660 --> 00:08:22,900
or have kind of bounced around from church to church, or if you spend a lot of time paying

128
00:08:22,900 --> 00:08:27,960
attention to American church trends, which I expect all of you to do, that's your job and

129
00:08:27,960 --> 00:08:28,720
your responsibility.

130
00:08:31,020 --> 00:08:35,360
No, obviously as a church leader, I spend a lot of time paying attention to church trends

131
00:08:35,360 --> 00:08:38,180
and what pastors are talking about all throughout the country.

132
00:08:38,180 --> 00:08:45,980
And you might know from that, you probably don't, but I'll tell you, that most pastors and kind

133
00:08:45,980 --> 00:08:50,260
of pollsters around the American church would say that the American church hasn't really figured

134
00:08:50,260 --> 00:08:52,560
out how to do both of these things well.

135
00:08:52,560 --> 00:08:57,720
There's a lot of churches that do numerical growth well, right?

136
00:08:57,720 --> 00:09:01,300
There's a lot of churches that are welcoming the outsider, that are building bigger buildings

137
00:09:01,300 --> 00:09:04,680
and inviting more and more people into their community.

138
00:09:04,680 --> 00:09:10,300
And there's a lot of churches that do spiritual maturity well, a lot of churches that are inviting

139
00:09:10,300 --> 00:09:13,280
believers to go deeper in their faith.

140
00:09:13,280 --> 00:09:17,880
But many churches have struggled to kind of blend this tension together.

141
00:09:17,880 --> 00:09:23,800
How do we focus on both those who aren't yet in the church building and mature those who already

142
00:09:23,800 --> 00:09:24,620
are?

143
00:09:24,700 --> 00:09:26,680
How do we do both of these things well?

144
00:09:26,680 --> 00:09:31,280
And because I know that most of you don't live in the church world, I want to bring this

145
00:09:31,280 --> 00:09:34,760
out of the church world and give you an illustration that I think helps a little bit.

146
00:09:34,760 --> 00:09:41,100
Since it's New Year's, and I know all of us have New Year's resolutions to lose the weight

147
00:09:41,100 --> 00:09:42,440
that we gained over Christmas break.

148
00:09:42,440 --> 00:09:44,080
Or is that just me?

149
00:09:44,080 --> 00:09:45,400
Okay, cool.

150
00:09:45,400 --> 00:09:46,280
It's just me.

151
00:09:46,720 --> 00:09:50,500
I watched a little bit of the ball drop, and I think it's really brilliant marketing.

152
00:09:50,500 --> 00:09:53,140
Do you see who sponsored the New Year's Eve ball drop?

153
00:09:53,140 --> 00:09:55,240
Planet Fitness, right?

154
00:09:55,240 --> 00:09:55,800
Yeah.

155
00:09:55,800 --> 00:09:57,780
It's good product placement.

156
00:09:57,780 --> 00:10:03,020
But when I think about this idea of kind of being welcome to the outsider and maturing

157
00:10:03,020 --> 00:10:06,780
the insider, I think about two different kind of gym franchises.

158
00:10:06,780 --> 00:10:11,440
We got Planet Fitness on one end, and the old Gold's Gym on the other end.

159
00:10:11,440 --> 00:10:11,860
Anybody?

160
00:10:11,860 --> 00:10:13,340
Yeah?

161
00:10:13,340 --> 00:10:19,200
Okay, so I've dabbled in taking care of myself physically a little bit, and I've been exposed

162
00:10:19,200 --> 00:10:20,600
to both of these organizations.

163
00:10:20,600 --> 00:10:24,340
And I don't know what your experience has been, but for what my experience has been, Planet Fitness

164
00:10:24,340 --> 00:10:26,280
is really good at welcoming the outsider.

165
00:10:26,280 --> 00:10:28,020
You haven't been to the gym in a year.

166
00:10:28,020 --> 00:10:29,600
You haven't been to the gym in your life.

167
00:10:29,600 --> 00:10:30,660
You are welcome here.

168
00:10:31,260 --> 00:10:35,460
$1 down, $10 a month, and you can just come in whenever you want.

169
00:10:35,460 --> 00:10:35,980
And guess what?

170
00:10:35,980 --> 00:10:38,200
If you don't come in, don't feel bad about yourself.

171
00:10:38,200 --> 00:10:40,060
Judgment-free zone, right?

172
00:10:40,060 --> 00:10:43,860
No lunks in here, lunk alarm.

173
00:10:43,860 --> 00:10:48,780
Like, you just don't actually worry about growing in fitness.

174
00:10:48,780 --> 00:10:51,880
Just, like, come in here when you get a chance.

175
00:10:51,880 --> 00:10:52,960
Feel good about yourself.

176
00:10:52,960 --> 00:10:57,220
And again, this is a very—these are hyper-generalizations.

177
00:10:57,220 --> 00:11:00,720
I was a member of Planet Fitness for a long time, and I actually did exercise.

178
00:11:00,720 --> 00:11:02,320
I'm not—there's no judgment here.

179
00:11:02,320 --> 00:11:06,520
I'm just trying to give you an illustration of how these two pendulums work, and it happens

180
00:11:06,520 --> 00:11:07,160
in our faith, too.

181
00:11:07,160 --> 00:11:13,120
And if you've ever been into a Gold's Gym, there's a lot of tank tops and protein shakes.

182
00:11:13,120 --> 00:11:18,440
Some veiny—some veiny individuals in there.

183
00:11:18,440 --> 00:11:25,580
And what Gold's Gym does not scream to me as I walk in is, like, hey, you don't know how

184
00:11:25,580 --> 00:11:26,220
to lift weights?

185
00:11:26,220 --> 00:11:27,720
You're welcome here.

186
00:11:27,720 --> 00:11:30,340
Come let us help you figure it out.

187
00:11:30,340 --> 00:11:33,220
We'll take you around to the machines that help you figure out the form.

188
00:11:33,220 --> 00:11:37,340
No, it's like, if you can't—if you can't get set up on a squat rack, then sorry about

189
00:11:37,340 --> 00:11:38,240
your bad luck, right?

190
00:11:38,240 --> 00:11:44,880
It's, like, so focused on strength and development and growth, and this side is so focused on

191
00:11:44,880 --> 00:11:47,180
welcome and invitational culture.

192
00:11:47,180 --> 00:11:51,820
And I think the church is—we have the tendency to swing the pendulum to one of those two things.

193
00:11:51,820 --> 00:11:55,980
We're either so welcoming to the outsider or we're so focused on maturing the insider

194
00:11:55,980 --> 00:12:03,020
that we aren't able to do what the early church did, which is grow in number daily and be strengthened

195
00:12:03,020 --> 00:12:03,660
in the faith.

196
00:12:03,720 --> 00:12:07,660
And I don't know about you, but I have a heart, a desire to be a part of a church that can

197
00:12:07,660 --> 00:12:08,820
do both of these things well.

198
00:12:08,820 --> 00:12:13,820
I don't want to forsake maturity for the sake of being a welcoming place for those who are

199
00:12:13,820 --> 00:12:14,860
outside the church.

200
00:12:14,860 --> 00:12:19,840
But I don't want to become so focused on maturity.

201
00:12:19,840 --> 00:12:23,500
I don't want to become so focused on those outside the church that we stop pushing each

202
00:12:23,500 --> 00:12:26,740
other to grow daily in our faith.

203
00:12:26,740 --> 00:12:29,640
So how do we do this?

204
00:12:29,640 --> 00:12:32,180
How do we become a church that does both well?

205
00:12:32,180 --> 00:12:36,340
How do we make room for people who are outside the church, inviting them in, and how do we

206
00:12:36,340 --> 00:12:42,500
make them feel welcome as they are while challenging anyone who wants to be inside the faith community

207
00:12:42,500 --> 00:12:47,820
to pursue maturity in Christ, to form a Christ-centered community?

208
00:12:48,720 --> 00:12:54,600
Well, I want to briefly look at the surrounding context of the verse and see what implications

209
00:12:54,600 --> 00:13:01,660
it might have for a church in America in 2026, which feels weird to say, so that we can see both

210
00:13:01,660 --> 00:13:04,600
numerical growth and spiritual maturity at the same time.

211
00:13:04,600 --> 00:13:05,940
Acts chapter 16.

212
00:13:05,940 --> 00:13:11,280
Let's zoom out a few verses and look at verse 4.

213
00:13:11,280 --> 00:13:13,940
All right, let's zoom out one verse.

214
00:13:13,940 --> 00:13:14,520
How about that?

215
00:13:16,040 --> 00:13:21,700
It says, as they traveled from town to town, they delivered the decisions reached by the

216
00:13:21,700 --> 00:13:26,040
apostles and elders in Jerusalem for the people to obey.

217
00:13:26,040 --> 00:13:31,980
So the churches were strengthened in faith and grew daily in number.

218
00:13:31,980 --> 00:13:38,860
What is this catalyst in the early church that resulted in the church both growing numerically

219
00:13:38,860 --> 00:13:40,700
and in maturity?

220
00:13:41,580 --> 00:13:47,360
Verse 4 tells us it was Paul and Silas and Timothy, and they were traveling around to the

221
00:13:47,360 --> 00:13:52,460
churches, and they were taking with them a decision delivered by the apostles and the elders.

222
00:13:52,460 --> 00:13:58,740
This decision, this statement from the church in Jerusalem is the thing that catapulted the

223
00:13:58,740 --> 00:14:03,960
churches to be both growing in number and maturing in faith.

224
00:14:04,480 --> 00:14:09,960
And if you grew up in church or you know your Bible well, you know that this decision that's

225
00:14:09,960 --> 00:14:12,500
being referred to is found in chapter 15.

226
00:14:12,500 --> 00:14:16,980
It's likely under the title in your Bible called the Jerusalem Council.

227
00:14:16,980 --> 00:14:22,220
This council was predicated upon the tension that I'm talking about.

228
00:14:22,220 --> 00:14:28,200
It was predicated behind the tension of the church growing in numbers and the church maturing

229
00:14:28,200 --> 00:14:28,680
in faith.

230
00:14:28,680 --> 00:14:33,960
See, when the church began in Acts chapter 2 at Pentecost, it was primarily Jewish people

231
00:14:33,960 --> 00:14:36,860
putting their faith in Jesus as the Messiah.

232
00:14:36,860 --> 00:14:40,540
We could call them, for our sake this morning, the insiders, right?

233
00:14:40,540 --> 00:14:46,600
These religious Jews had heard Peter proclaim the gospel of Jesus, and they accepted it by faith.

234
00:14:46,600 --> 00:14:52,100
They received the Holy Spirit, and they were the foundation of the early church.

235
00:14:53,400 --> 00:14:58,180
The early, the spiritually mature of the early church were primarily religious Jews.

236
00:14:58,180 --> 00:15:04,620
But as the gospel advanced, just as Jesus said it would, from Jerusalem to Judea to Samaria to the

237
00:15:04,620 --> 00:15:10,800
ends of the known world, the Holy Spirit began to fill Gentiles just as he did the Jews.

238
00:15:10,800 --> 00:15:15,480
Gentiles were coming to faith in Jesus in large numbers and joining the church.

239
00:15:15,480 --> 00:15:20,420
So we have spiritually mature insiders who are the foundation of the church, and now we have

240
00:15:20,420 --> 00:15:27,540
Gentiles who are culturally so far away from the Jews now coming into the same church, being welcomed

241
00:15:27,540 --> 00:15:31,320
and receiving the same Holy Spirit, and we can feel this tension here.

242
00:15:31,320 --> 00:15:39,360
This caused many Jews to have lots of problems, and a number of Jewish Christians started going

243
00:15:39,360 --> 00:15:44,800
out of Jerusalem from church to church and telling all of the Gentiles that if they wanted to be

244
00:15:44,800 --> 00:15:50,500
welcome in the church, if they wanted to be a part of what God was doing through Jesus, they

245
00:15:50,500 --> 00:15:58,920
had to obey all of the laws of Moses and, drumroll please, be circumcised.

246
00:15:58,920 --> 00:16:01,460
Super welcoming, right?

247
00:16:01,460 --> 00:16:03,100
Super inviting.

248
00:16:03,100 --> 00:16:05,460
Gentlemen, you want to follow Jesus?

249
00:16:05,460 --> 00:16:06,920
You want to be a part of Canvas Community Church?

250
00:16:06,920 --> 00:16:07,660
Minor surgery?

251
00:16:07,660 --> 00:16:08,500
Major surgery?

252
00:16:08,500 --> 00:16:09,360
Let's do it.

253
00:16:09,360 --> 00:16:12,560
Can you feel the tension, right?

254
00:16:13,120 --> 00:16:17,820
Insiders are trying to require their definition of spiritual maturity on the outsiders.

255
00:16:17,820 --> 00:16:24,100
Meanwhile, there are pagan outsiders who have spent their whole life in idolatry and immorality,

256
00:16:24,100 --> 00:16:28,120
wanting to forsake that life and follow Jesus and be a part of the church.

257
00:16:28,120 --> 00:16:30,620
There's this tension here.

258
00:16:30,620 --> 00:16:35,020
Outsiders are coming in, and they're coming in with all of their baggage, with all of their

259
00:16:35,020 --> 00:16:38,900
immorality and idolatry, but they're saying, we see beauty in Jesus.

260
00:16:38,900 --> 00:16:43,920
We see that the world thinks the cross is foolishness, but we see that it is the power of God to bring

261
00:16:43,920 --> 00:16:45,660
salvation, and we want a part of this.

262
00:16:45,660 --> 00:16:52,020
And the insiders are like, okay, you got some hoops to jump through, some hurdles to overcome.

263
00:16:52,020 --> 00:16:56,620
You've got to become like us to be welcomed into our space.

264
00:16:56,620 --> 00:17:02,840
And so this kind of tension that was happening is why the elders and the apostles met in chapter

265
00:17:02,840 --> 00:17:09,080
15, and ultimately they came to a very important decision at the early church that we're going

266
00:17:09,080 --> 00:17:11,800
to read, and you are going to be confused, and that is okay.

267
00:17:11,800 --> 00:17:13,160
We will unpack it, okay?

268
00:17:13,160 --> 00:17:20,540
So tension between outsiders coming in, insiders wanting outsiders to look like them and be

269
00:17:20,540 --> 00:17:21,820
mature by their definition.

270
00:17:21,820 --> 00:17:23,920
And they're fighting over this.

271
00:17:23,920 --> 00:17:24,860
What does this look like?

272
00:17:24,900 --> 00:17:30,160
And this is the letter that Paul and Timothy and Silas are taking to the churches, and

273
00:17:30,160 --> 00:17:37,340
this, believe it or not, believe it or not, this letter is the catalyst for the church growing

274
00:17:37,340 --> 00:17:40,840
in number and being strengthened in faith daily.

275
00:17:40,840 --> 00:17:43,680
And I like to be a humble person.

276
00:17:45,960 --> 00:17:49,700
I'd like to think I've preached some better sermons than what you're going to read in this letter.

277
00:17:49,700 --> 00:17:52,640
It'll make sense when we get there.

278
00:17:52,640 --> 00:17:59,780
But this letter is the catalyst for the church, both growing in number and strengthening in faith.

279
00:17:59,780 --> 00:18:04,400
It says in chapter 15, verse 23, with them, they sent the following letter to the apostles,

280
00:18:04,400 --> 00:18:10,260
the apostles and elders, your brothers, to the Gentile believers in Antioch, Syria, and Cilicia.

281
00:18:10,720 --> 00:18:17,300
Greetings, we have heard that some went out from us without our authorization and disturbed

282
00:18:17,300 --> 00:18:19,180
you, troubling your minds by what they said.

283
00:18:19,180 --> 00:18:25,480
So we all agreed to choose some men and send them to you with our dear friends Barnabas and

284
00:18:25,480 --> 00:18:29,540
Paul, men who have risked their lives for the name of the Lord Jesus Christ.

285
00:18:29,540 --> 00:18:35,180
Therefore, we are sending Judas and Silas to confirm by word of mouth what we are writing.

286
00:18:35,180 --> 00:18:38,780
This is the powerful sermon that changed everything for the early church.

287
00:18:38,780 --> 00:18:43,960
It seemed good to the Holy Spirit and to us, not to burden you with anything beyond the following

288
00:18:43,960 --> 00:18:44,600
requirements.

289
00:18:44,600 --> 00:18:50,840
You are to abstain from food sacrifice to idols, from blood, from the meat of strangled animals,

290
00:18:50,840 --> 00:18:52,340
and from sexual immorality.

291
00:18:52,340 --> 00:18:54,480
You will do well to avoid these things.

292
00:18:54,480 --> 00:18:55,040
Farewell.

293
00:18:55,040 --> 00:19:01,320
Have I at least preached one or two sermons that's a little bit better than that?

294
00:19:01,320 --> 00:19:02,600
Okay, thank you.

295
00:19:02,600 --> 00:19:03,460
I appreciate that.

296
00:19:03,460 --> 00:19:08,420
So there's a lot going on here in this brief letter.

297
00:19:08,420 --> 00:19:11,800
There are a lot of cultural things that we don't have all of the time to unpack, but

298
00:19:11,800 --> 00:19:16,540
I think there are three key principles that we see in this letter from the early church

299
00:19:16,540 --> 00:19:22,280
that became the catalyst for a church that was able to grow both numerically and in spiritual

300
00:19:22,280 --> 00:19:26,080
maturity, a church that was able to hold these two things in tension.

301
00:19:26,080 --> 00:19:28,500
That's what I want to spend the rest of our time talking about.

302
00:19:28,500 --> 00:19:31,140
Longest introduction you've ever heard.

303
00:19:31,140 --> 00:19:31,980
You guys did great.

304
00:19:31,980 --> 00:19:32,840
All right, let's get started.

305
00:19:32,940 --> 00:19:40,060
Number one, if we want to be a church that both grows numerically and in spiritual maturity,

306
00:19:40,060 --> 00:19:44,020
we need to be a church that removes unnecessary barriers for those on the outside.

307
00:19:44,020 --> 00:19:50,000
The early church leaders who, by the way, were very Jewish.

308
00:19:50,320 --> 00:19:54,200
These apostles and elders in Jerusalem were about as Jewish as they come.

309
00:19:54,200 --> 00:20:00,240
And when this argument was brought to their table, they completely shot down the idea of

310
00:20:00,240 --> 00:20:03,840
Gentiles having to become Jewish in order to be welcomed into the church.

311
00:20:03,840 --> 00:20:08,720
You will see in this letter, no mention of circumcision, no mention of obedience to the Mosaic law,

312
00:20:08,720 --> 00:20:11,660
just faith in Christ and a few weird things.

313
00:20:11,660 --> 00:20:14,420
That we'll unpack why in a little bit later.

314
00:20:15,700 --> 00:20:20,420
So the church in this letter immediately is signaling to the Gentiles and to the Jews

315
00:20:20,420 --> 00:20:26,040
that we are going to be a church that removes unnecessary barriers for those who are outside the church.

316
00:20:26,040 --> 00:20:28,600
It's just about faith in Jesus.

317
00:20:28,600 --> 00:20:31,300
And a reminder, we've talked about this a lot over the past few years,

318
00:20:31,300 --> 00:20:33,220
but faith is not intellectual agreement.

319
00:20:33,220 --> 00:20:36,240
Faith in this first century world is allegiance.

320
00:20:36,240 --> 00:20:40,000
Think about, I pledge allegiance to the United States of America.

321
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:41,880
When the early church read faith, they would think,

322
00:20:41,880 --> 00:20:45,660
I pledge allegiance to the crucified and risen Savior, Jesus Christ.

323
00:20:46,140 --> 00:20:51,380
So if you want to do that, if you want to, I surrender all, I lay my life down at the feet of Jesus,

324
00:20:51,380 --> 00:20:52,100
you are welcome.

325
00:20:52,100 --> 00:20:59,720
And you do not have to overcome cultural barriers to get here and be a part of who we are and what we're doing.

326
00:20:59,720 --> 00:21:03,720
Now, obviously, at least I hope it's obvious,

327
00:21:03,720 --> 00:21:08,340
circumcision and obedience to the Mosaic law is not something that churches

328
00:21:08,340 --> 00:21:12,300
who are focused on maturity are still demanding today.

329
00:21:13,680 --> 00:21:17,180
But we do have our own version of this.

330
00:21:17,180 --> 00:21:23,840
Many churches, who I would call the Gold's Gym churches, right,

331
00:21:23,840 --> 00:21:27,300
who are really focused on strengthening and maturing the faith of the believers,

332
00:21:27,300 --> 00:21:33,300
many churches who focus on maturity have created their own version of barriers and obstacles

333
00:21:33,300 --> 00:21:34,780
for those outside the church.

334
00:21:35,260 --> 00:21:42,260
There is an American Christian subculture that has identified itself with a lot of things

335
00:21:42,260 --> 00:21:44,760
in addition to allegiance to King Jesus.

336
00:21:44,760 --> 00:21:50,800
And sometimes, sometimes these things are even opposed to the kingdom of God.

337
00:21:50,800 --> 00:21:56,820
But we got really churchy ways of like camouflaging it and making it seem like it's not opposed,

338
00:21:56,820 --> 00:21:58,340
but they are.

339
00:21:58,980 --> 00:22:04,180
We have these subtle ways of telling outsiders that if they want to be fully embraced into the church,

340
00:22:04,180 --> 00:22:06,340
they have to assimilate.

341
00:22:06,340 --> 00:22:11,440
They have to look like us and talk like us and act like us.

342
00:22:11,440 --> 00:22:15,580
It's the Gold's Gym effect, right?

343
00:22:15,580 --> 00:22:17,460
Like, yeah, sure, come on in, but look around.

344
00:22:17,460 --> 00:22:19,580
Look at what we value.

345
00:22:19,580 --> 00:22:21,080
Look at what we look like.

346
00:22:21,080 --> 00:22:22,120
Figure out what we do.

347
00:22:22,140 --> 00:22:24,840
And if you want to be welcomed here, you've got to be like us.

348
00:22:24,840 --> 00:22:30,420
We'll get to this a little bit more later, but for now, what I want to say

349
00:22:30,420 --> 00:22:34,540
is that if we want to be a church who grows numerically and matures in faith,

350
00:22:34,540 --> 00:22:38,660
we need to be a church that removes unnecessary barriers for those outside the church.

351
00:22:38,660 --> 00:22:43,140
And if you're here and you're not really a church person,

352
00:22:43,140 --> 00:22:45,560
or at least you haven't been a church person in a long time,

353
00:22:45,560 --> 00:22:51,060
we want this to be a safe place where you are welcome to explore allegiance to Jesus as King.

354
00:22:51,640 --> 00:22:55,220
This is not an insignificant decision.

355
00:22:55,220 --> 00:22:57,420
So come as you are.

356
00:22:57,420 --> 00:22:58,120
You are welcome.

357
00:22:58,120 --> 00:23:01,140
You do not have to adopt some type of weird subcultural identity.

358
00:23:01,140 --> 00:23:04,160
You don't have to agree with everything that we say and do,

359
00:23:04,160 --> 00:23:08,400
and you are free to wrestle and ask questions and challenge the status quo.

360
00:23:08,400 --> 00:23:11,160
We want this to be a safe place where you can do that.

361
00:23:11,160 --> 00:23:14,900
It reminds me of Christ's interaction with a few of his disciples.

362
00:23:14,900 --> 00:23:20,980
We often focus on this invitation that Jesus gives his disciples to lay down their life and follow him,

363
00:23:21,140 --> 00:23:23,520
to take up their cross, to lay down everything they have.

364
00:23:23,520 --> 00:23:25,340
But before that, for a number of his disciples,

365
00:23:25,500 --> 00:23:28,400
Jesus gave this invitation where he just said, come and see.

366
00:23:28,400 --> 00:23:30,800
Come and see where I live.

367
00:23:30,800 --> 00:23:33,580
Come and see the rhythms of my life.

368
00:23:33,580 --> 00:23:35,460
Just come be with me for a while.

369
00:23:35,460 --> 00:23:39,540
And for many of the people that ended up giving their life for Jesus,

370
00:23:39,540 --> 00:23:45,840
their conversion didn't start with some Damascus road, everything's gone, I'm all in with Jesus.

371
00:23:45,960 --> 00:23:51,480
Their conversion started with just hanging around them, just orbiting around the Savior.

372
00:23:51,480 --> 00:23:56,440
And so we want to be a church where if you're spiritually curious, you can come in here,

373
00:23:56,440 --> 00:24:00,200
and you are welcome to come as you are with all of your baggage, with all of your questions,

374
00:24:00,200 --> 00:24:02,380
with all of whatever you have going on.

375
00:24:03,060 --> 00:24:06,880
Come and see what it is like to place your faith and trust in Jesus.

376
00:24:12,440 --> 00:24:16,920
So, we want to be a church that removes unnecessary barriers.

377
00:24:16,920 --> 00:24:23,040
However, there will come a point in time for all of us, both those inside the church and outside the church,

378
00:24:23,040 --> 00:24:27,760
where our spiritual journeys come face to face with a crossroad with Jesus.

379
00:24:28,620 --> 00:24:36,440
There will come a point where you and I and all of us will have to choose if we are going to walk down the narrow road of Jesus Christ

380
00:24:36,440 --> 00:24:42,600
that leads to life, or if we are going to choose to walk down the wide road that leads to destruction.

381
00:24:42,600 --> 00:24:45,940
So, we want to be a church that removes unnecessary barriers.

382
00:24:45,940 --> 00:24:48,100
You're welcome to come as you are, ask questions, wrestle.

383
00:24:48,100 --> 00:24:53,040
But at some point in time, you are going to have to decide if you want to follow Jesus or not.

384
00:24:55,580 --> 00:25:00,960
Which leads me to our second principle that I find, that I think we find in this letter, and that's this.

385
00:25:00,960 --> 00:25:06,680
A church that lovingly calls outsiders to leave behind cultural values for kingdom values.

386
00:25:06,680 --> 00:25:15,700
Now, I acknowledge, as we read here, that these four prohibitions given to the Gentiles seem a bit odd to us.

387
00:25:15,700 --> 00:25:18,340
And there's a lot of scholarly debate.

388
00:25:18,340 --> 00:25:23,820
I read a number of different commentaries this week, and there's some overlapping agreement and some disagreement.

389
00:25:24,220 --> 00:25:31,000
But at the end of the day, for our purposes this morning, one of the main overarching ideas that scholars agreed with,

390
00:25:31,000 --> 00:25:37,000
pretty much across the board, is that these prohibitions were given to help guard those who are outside the church,

391
00:25:37,000 --> 00:25:44,100
these Gentile Christians coming in, from polluting the gospel with the prevailing idolatry of their Roman world.

392
00:25:44,100 --> 00:25:49,540
Most of these Gentile Christians would have been discipled by birth in Roman idolatry.

393
00:25:49,940 --> 00:25:51,780
This was the water that they swam in.

394
00:25:51,780 --> 00:25:53,680
They didn't even realize that it was a religion.

395
00:25:53,680 --> 00:25:56,280
It was just the culture that they lived in.

396
00:25:56,280 --> 00:26:03,660
Much of this Roman idolatry included eating meat sacrificed to idols, the consumption of blood, and cult prostitution.

397
00:26:03,660 --> 00:26:10,020
Many of their jobs and their relationships would have required them to participate in these practices.

398
00:26:10,160 --> 00:26:13,520
There would have been a ton of pressure from the outside world.

399
00:26:13,520 --> 00:26:20,280
Even as they were coming to faith in Jesus, there would have been a ton of cultural pressure to just live in the Roman waters,

400
00:26:20,280 --> 00:26:23,580
to do the things that Romans do.

401
00:26:23,680 --> 00:26:27,940
And the decision by the early church is inviting those who are coming into the church from the outside

402
00:26:27,940 --> 00:26:36,120
to see the ways in which the values of their culture come face-to-face in confrontation with the values of the kingdom of God.

403
00:26:39,720 --> 00:26:45,720
The early church is encouraging them to see their culturally inherited values in opposition to the values of God's kingdom.

404
00:26:45,720 --> 00:26:53,800
Where there is no other God but Jesus, and those who seek to worship him will do so in spirit and in truth.

405
00:26:55,140 --> 00:27:00,980
They wanted the outsiders to be welcome to come and see, and ultimately to become followers of Jesus without becoming Jewish.

406
00:27:00,980 --> 00:27:06,680
But they did not hide the fact that following Jesus would put them at odds with the values of their culture.

407
00:27:06,680 --> 00:27:11,680
At odds with the values of those they live, work, and play with.

408
00:27:11,680 --> 00:27:14,800
And we want to be a church who does the same.

409
00:27:14,800 --> 00:27:18,460
You don't have to become some weird Christian subculture to follow Jesus,

410
00:27:18,460 --> 00:27:21,660
but you do have to embrace the values of the kingdom of God.

411
00:27:22,800 --> 00:27:24,880
This is not an option.

412
00:27:24,880 --> 00:27:25,640
Allegiance.

413
00:27:25,640 --> 00:27:31,580
Faith is allegiance, and allegiance to Jesus means his kingdom values trump our culture's values.

414
00:27:31,580 --> 00:27:35,160
I want us to be a church that does this.

415
00:27:35,160 --> 00:27:38,100
Where you can come as you are and explore faith,

416
00:27:38,100 --> 00:27:43,600
but also we want to be clear that if you choose to embrace the kingdom of God through Jesus,

417
00:27:43,600 --> 00:27:47,200
the values of the kingdom will directly confront the values of our culture.

418
00:27:47,200 --> 00:27:49,260
We're going to spend more time on that next week.

419
00:27:49,260 --> 00:27:51,360
That's exactly the 1 Corinthians passage we read.

420
00:27:52,000 --> 00:27:53,560
It's foolishness to the world.

421
00:27:53,560 --> 00:27:59,580
The cross of Jesus, the kingdom of God is foolishness to the world around us.

422
00:27:59,580 --> 00:28:04,720
It's foolishness to religious people who are in here pretending to be kingdom people.

423
00:28:04,720 --> 00:28:14,580
This is the Planet Fitness thing, right?

424
00:28:14,620 --> 00:28:21,780
Like, we want you to be welcome, but we don't want to create such an environment where we don't even hold you accountable or care that you don't work out, right?

425
00:28:21,780 --> 00:28:27,420
It's like, hey, I come here twice a month for an hour and a half, and I peace out, and like, I'm good.

426
00:28:27,420 --> 00:28:28,480
I feel good about myself.

427
00:28:28,480 --> 00:28:29,120
I'm spiritual.

428
00:28:29,120 --> 00:28:38,320
It's like, no, as you come in here, you will be confronted with the fact that your life is built on cultural values that are ultimately in opposition to the values of the kingdom of God,

429
00:28:38,320 --> 00:28:40,080
and you cannot do both.

430
00:28:40,080 --> 00:28:45,400
At some point in time, you will have to decide the wide road or the narrow road.

431
00:28:46,560 --> 00:28:51,540
Now, our culture doesn't overtly practice idol worship or cult prostitution, right?

432
00:28:51,540 --> 00:28:54,820
Well, yeah, all right, there we go.

433
00:28:54,820 --> 00:28:55,300
I got it.

434
00:28:55,300 --> 00:28:55,920
We'll get there.

435
00:28:55,920 --> 00:29:05,580
In the same way that they were addressing in Acts chapter 16, but I will say, and I could say a number of things.

436
00:29:05,580 --> 00:29:12,560
I thought about listing, like, 15 cultural values that all go in, like, contradiction to the values of the kingdom,

437
00:29:12,560 --> 00:29:15,780
but I just picked two because I was feeling pastorally kind.

438
00:29:15,780 --> 00:29:26,340
So, two cultural values that we, as American Christians, deal with on a regular basis.

439
00:29:26,340 --> 00:29:27,860
This is the water that we swim in.

440
00:29:27,860 --> 00:29:33,820
Many of us don't even realize that these values are ultimately in blatant contradiction to the values of the kingdom of God.

441
00:29:33,820 --> 00:29:35,040
First is individualism.

442
00:29:35,040 --> 00:29:39,300
My life is ultimately about me and my happiness.

443
00:29:39,300 --> 00:29:41,440
This is the number one American ideal.

444
00:29:42,080 --> 00:29:43,240
This is the American dream.

445
00:29:43,240 --> 00:29:46,860
I have the ability to pick myself up by the bootstraps.

446
00:29:46,860 --> 00:29:49,800
I can have everything I want in this world.

447
00:29:49,800 --> 00:29:51,200
Do not tell me what to do.

448
00:29:51,200 --> 00:29:55,980
To put it another way, don't tread on me.

449
00:29:55,980 --> 00:29:58,440
Anybody got that on their license plate?

450
00:29:58,440 --> 00:30:00,100
I'm not convicting you for that.

451
00:30:00,100 --> 00:30:06,320
Just saying, this is the ideal individualism that's built into our culture.

452
00:30:06,320 --> 00:30:08,860
It's the water we swim in and we don't even realize it.

453
00:30:09,540 --> 00:30:13,760
And the value of the kingdom of God is actually about giving up your life for the sake of others.

454
00:30:14,360 --> 00:30:17,220
It's about enemy, love, and surrender.

455
00:30:17,220 --> 00:30:27,900
So individualism, one of these things that we can invite those who are coming outside from outside the church and those of us who are inside the church to realize that these are cultural values in the waters that we swim.

456
00:30:27,900 --> 00:30:35,340
And second, happiness is found through material possessions, experiences, and financial security.

457
00:30:35,340 --> 00:30:41,440
And I have to do whatever it takes to ensure that I have those things and that those I love have those things.

458
00:30:42,240 --> 00:30:52,040
And pastorally, I just have to remind you this morning, just because I spent some of my time off taking a bunch of old stuff to the dumps so that I had room for all of the new stuff.

459
00:30:52,040 --> 00:30:59,420
The stuff that you are chasing, the stuff that I am chasing is stuff of next year's garage sale.

460
00:30:59,420 --> 00:31:02,120
It will not satisfy the void in your soul.

461
00:31:02,620 --> 00:31:09,780
And we live and we swim in these cultural waters that just says that this is the thing that we should be chasing.

462
00:31:09,780 --> 00:31:13,700
And Ecclesiastes says it's like grasping at the wind.

463
00:31:13,700 --> 00:31:14,960
You're never going to find it.

464
00:31:16,580 --> 00:31:20,760
The kingdom of God says the stuff you are chasing is the stuff of next year's garage sale.

465
00:31:20,760 --> 00:31:25,520
Everything you have is a gift from God and you are invited to use it for the good of those around you.

466
00:31:25,520 --> 00:31:27,480
And again, I'm going to keep coming back to it.

467
00:31:27,480 --> 00:31:31,540
This is what Paul says in 1 Corinthians is foolishness to the world.

468
00:31:31,540 --> 00:31:38,700
Tell your neighbor who's not interested in Jesus that your pastor spent an hour telling you that everything you have is somebody else's

469
00:31:38,700 --> 00:31:40,960
and that you should lay down your preferences for the good of others.

470
00:31:40,960 --> 00:31:47,500
And they're going to say, run away because he is going to send your life on a downward spiral.

471
00:31:47,500 --> 00:31:51,740
But ultimately, Jesus says this is where abundant life is found.

472
00:31:51,740 --> 00:31:55,840
We want to be a church that welcomes everyone to come as they are and to wrestle.

473
00:31:55,840 --> 00:32:02,500
But we will unapologetically invite you to lay down the values of this culture for the values of the kingdom of God,

474
00:32:02,500 --> 00:32:04,520
even when it seems foolish.

475
00:32:07,480 --> 00:32:14,980
And at the end of the day, this way of preaching, many, many American pastors,

476
00:32:14,980 --> 00:32:18,780
many of those who are really good at growing their church in numbers,

477
00:32:18,780 --> 00:32:25,480
would kind of shy away from this level of messaging because they'll say that's going to run people off.

478
00:32:25,480 --> 00:32:27,500
You got to make them feel more welcome.

479
00:32:27,500 --> 00:32:31,340
And then eventually we'll get to the whole surrender to the King Jesus thing.

480
00:32:32,240 --> 00:32:36,700
And what I'm going to say is at some point, these messages where we're talking about leaving,

481
00:32:36,700 --> 00:32:40,880
forsaking the values of the culture for the values of the kingdom, it might run people off.

482
00:32:40,880 --> 00:32:43,920
But in the end, it will still lead to more growth.

483
00:32:43,920 --> 00:32:50,840
Because the church, the world outside is looking for a church that is effective at living the kingdom values.

484
00:32:50,840 --> 00:32:54,760
And if we keep inviting people in and telling them that it's okay to stay as they are,

485
00:32:54,760 --> 00:32:56,960
they don't have to embrace the values of the kingdom of God,

486
00:32:57,060 --> 00:33:00,500
then the world outside will look at the church and say, you guys are no different than us.

487
00:33:00,500 --> 00:33:03,360
You guys look exactly like us.

488
00:33:03,360 --> 00:33:05,520
Why would we have anything to do with the message?

489
00:33:05,520 --> 00:33:09,320
You guys just waste the whole Sunday morning and you don't look any different than we do.

490
00:33:09,320 --> 00:33:16,220
I just heard Craig Rochelle give a sermon a couple weeks ago about, I won't get into it,

491
00:33:16,220 --> 00:33:19,360
but he said like, so many parents just want their kids to be normal.

492
00:33:19,360 --> 00:33:22,460
And he said, look what normal looks like.

493
00:33:23,880 --> 00:33:26,560
A little weird is not all bad.

494
00:33:26,560 --> 00:33:30,320
And this is what the church does when we hold people accountable,

495
00:33:30,320 --> 00:33:34,120
when we invite people to lay down normal for the sake of the kingdom,

496
00:33:34,120 --> 00:33:37,740
is we become a more effective gospel witness to the world around us.

497
00:33:37,740 --> 00:33:39,140
Because we do look a little weird,

498
00:33:39,140 --> 00:33:42,800
but the little weird that we look like is a little attractive to the world who is exhausted,

499
00:33:42,800 --> 00:33:46,740
who is brokenhearted, who is downtrodden,

500
00:33:46,740 --> 00:33:49,580
who is trying to find hope in material things,

501
00:33:49,580 --> 00:33:52,500
who is trying to find hope in all of the things that the world has to offer,

502
00:33:52,500 --> 00:33:54,080
and they can't find it.

503
00:33:54,080 --> 00:33:57,440
So it might run some people off,

504
00:33:57,440 --> 00:33:58,260
but at the end of the day,

505
00:33:58,260 --> 00:34:01,720
it will still produce a church that grows spiritually and numerically,

506
00:34:01,720 --> 00:34:03,800
because this is how the kingdom works.

507
00:34:03,800 --> 00:34:06,040
This is the power of God to bring salvation.

508
00:34:06,040 --> 00:34:13,180
Oh, last one.

509
00:34:15,360 --> 00:34:16,040
Number three.

510
00:34:16,040 --> 00:34:16,060
Number three.

511
00:34:16,060 --> 00:34:18,620
And this is kind of a mirror of the first two,

512
00:34:18,620 --> 00:34:21,160
more applied to insiders of the church than outsiders.

513
00:34:21,160 --> 00:34:22,640
I probably could have made it two points,

514
00:34:22,640 --> 00:34:25,580
but as you can tell, I'm not good at managing my time.

515
00:34:25,580 --> 00:34:27,300
So number three.

516
00:34:27,300 --> 00:34:32,020
A church that continually reorients insiders away from religious values

517
00:34:32,020 --> 00:34:33,960
and back to the kingdom truth.

518
00:34:35,960 --> 00:34:42,380
What the elders and apostles did when they totally forsook circumcision

519
00:34:42,380 --> 00:34:45,760
and obedience to the Mosaic law is they were telling these Jewish Christians

520
00:34:45,760 --> 00:34:48,600
who thought, man, Jesus is the Jewish Messiah.

521
00:34:48,600 --> 00:34:50,500
If you want to follow him, you've got to become Jewish.

522
00:34:50,500 --> 00:34:54,760
They were mistakenly blending their national and cultural values

523
00:34:54,760 --> 00:34:55,760
with the values of the kingdom.

524
00:34:55,760 --> 00:34:58,800
And the early church leader says, we're not going to do that.

525
00:34:58,800 --> 00:35:03,720
We're not going to make people become ethnically like us to follow our Savior,

526
00:35:03,720 --> 00:35:05,880
because that's not what Jesus was inviting people to.

527
00:35:05,880 --> 00:35:10,440
You read the prophets all throughout this imagery that we often think is like

528
00:35:10,440 --> 00:35:13,040
second coming stuff is actually first coming stuff.

529
00:35:13,040 --> 00:35:17,020
And it's all about all of the nations being welcomed to follow Jesus.

530
00:35:17,020 --> 00:35:22,580
That this is the kingdom of God coming on earth as it is in heaven.

531
00:35:22,580 --> 00:35:24,380
And the early Jews were missing it.

532
00:35:24,380 --> 00:35:26,300
They were saying, no, you've got to become like us.

533
00:35:26,300 --> 00:35:28,660
You've got to have our values.

534
00:35:28,660 --> 00:35:31,000
You've got to have our religion.

535
00:35:31,720 --> 00:35:35,920
They mistakenly blended their national and cultural values to the values of the kingdom of God.

536
00:35:35,920 --> 00:35:39,280
And this decision by their leaders directly confronted that.

537
00:35:39,280 --> 00:35:43,560
And I believe if we want to be a church that grows numerically and spiritually,

538
00:35:43,560 --> 00:35:44,560
we have to do the same.

539
00:35:44,560 --> 00:35:47,440
We have to take those of you who have been inside the church for a long time.

540
00:35:47,440 --> 00:35:54,040
And you've blended what it means to be a Christian with other moral, religious,

541
00:35:54,040 --> 00:35:56,300
and Christian subcultural stuff.

542
00:35:56,880 --> 00:36:01,100
And you have identified that as the core tenets of Christianity.

543
00:36:01,100 --> 00:36:04,380
And so when outsiders come in and they want to surrender their life to King Jesus,

544
00:36:04,380 --> 00:36:07,840
but they don't watch the same news channel you watch.

545
00:36:07,840 --> 00:36:10,740
They don't listen to the same music that you listen to.

546
00:36:10,740 --> 00:36:12,540
They don't wear the same clothes that you wear.

547
00:36:12,540 --> 00:36:16,840
You're a little bit afraid about your kids hanging out with their kids.

548
00:36:18,120 --> 00:36:22,720
And what you end up doing is because you're an insider and you have a little bit of privilege

549
00:36:22,720 --> 00:36:25,840
and a little bit of power, as you look at them and subtly tell them,

550
00:36:25,840 --> 00:36:29,660
hey, if you want to be a part of our community, you've got to assimilate.

551
00:36:29,660 --> 00:36:30,880
You've got to look like me.

552
00:36:30,880 --> 00:36:32,000
You've got to talk like me.

553
00:36:32,000 --> 00:36:37,700
You've got to believe some of the nuanced secondary theology that I believe.

554
00:36:38,580 --> 00:36:42,000
And I think if we're going to be a church that grows in number and maturity,

555
00:36:42,000 --> 00:36:48,960
we have to tell the insiders when they are embracing national and religious identities

556
00:36:48,960 --> 00:36:51,360
and values over the kingdom of God.

557
00:36:51,360 --> 00:36:57,140
And many of us inside the church have tacked on our cultural Christian values to the kingdom.

558
00:36:57,140 --> 00:37:02,380
And I want to be a church that constantly challenges insiders to reorient themselves

559
00:37:02,380 --> 00:37:07,160
to the values of the kingdom and leave behind any additional values that they tack on to the gospel

560
00:37:07,160 --> 00:37:11,520
and require other people to surrender to in order to feel welcome.

561
00:37:11,520 --> 00:37:16,260
And rabbit trail, this makes unity hard.

562
00:37:16,260 --> 00:37:21,700
It's much easier to have unity when everybody looks the same, watches the same news channel

563
00:37:21,700 --> 00:37:24,660
and all of that stuff.

564
00:37:24,660 --> 00:37:29,940
But also it's a type of unity that the world outside looks in and says,

565
00:37:29,940 --> 00:37:31,380
yeah, we have that unity too.

566
00:37:31,380 --> 00:37:35,060
We can unify with people that believe the same things we believe,

567
00:37:35,060 --> 00:37:39,420
we've talked the same way we talk, have the same voting record that we have, all of that stuff.

568
00:37:39,420 --> 00:37:44,420
And the thing that makes the church the good news, the gospel to the watching world,

569
00:37:44,420 --> 00:37:49,560
is that we can have unity in the midst of diversity and disagreement

570
00:37:49,560 --> 00:37:54,420
because of the values of the kingdom of God are ultimately our primary values.

571
00:37:55,120 --> 00:37:58,080
I love how Sky Jethani says that he says,

572
00:37:58,080 --> 00:38:03,940
if you have constructed a vision of Christianity that says heaven's favor, abundance, and blessing

573
00:38:03,940 --> 00:38:07,200
are exclusively for people that look like you,

574
00:38:07,200 --> 00:38:11,620
that's a sure sign that your imagination has been shaped more by the divisions of the world

575
00:38:11,620 --> 00:38:13,180
than by the kingdom of heaven.

576
00:38:14,520 --> 00:38:18,460
And so insiders, those of you that have been in church for a long time,

577
00:38:18,460 --> 00:38:21,060
I want you to take some inventory of your life,

578
00:38:21,060 --> 00:38:24,180
some inventory of the things that you would consider kingdom values.

579
00:38:24,180 --> 00:38:27,340
Are they actually values of the kingdom of God?

580
00:38:27,340 --> 00:38:31,920
Or are they values of some weird American Christian subculture that you have created,

581
00:38:31,920 --> 00:38:35,460
that we have created, and we have identified as Christianity?

582
00:38:37,420 --> 00:38:40,360
Because I want us to go back to the Sermon on the Mount a little bit,

583
00:38:40,360 --> 00:38:43,520
read Matthew 5-7, and think, is the type of Christianity,

584
00:38:43,520 --> 00:38:46,640
the American Christianity that we've created and said, this is Christianity.

585
00:38:46,640 --> 00:38:51,820
Now, think about those things, and think about the Sermon on the Mount,

586
00:38:51,820 --> 00:38:56,280
the key teaching of Jesus about the kingdom of God,

587
00:38:56,280 --> 00:38:58,020
and where do those two things line up,

588
00:38:58,020 --> 00:39:00,440
and where are they in contradiction with one another?

589
00:39:00,440 --> 00:39:05,700
Toe stepped, all right.

590
00:39:08,280 --> 00:39:10,180
Second, so we got nationalism,

591
00:39:10,180 --> 00:39:13,760
where we kind of identify this version of American Christianity

592
00:39:13,760 --> 00:39:18,740
as a very niche, small-minded idea of Christianity,

593
00:39:18,740 --> 00:39:21,020
and we make everyone else come and look like us,

594
00:39:21,020 --> 00:39:23,300
and talk like us, and act like us, and believe like us.

595
00:39:23,300 --> 00:39:27,780
This is going to halt the church from growing spiritually in a number.

596
00:39:27,780 --> 00:39:28,940
And the second is moralism.

597
00:39:28,940 --> 00:39:32,640
It's this idea that if you want to be an insider,

598
00:39:32,640 --> 00:39:36,960
you have to prioritize external obedience to a list of sins

599
00:39:36,960 --> 00:39:37,820
that we don't like.

600
00:39:37,820 --> 00:39:42,880
That that becomes the primary thought of the kingdom,

601
00:39:42,880 --> 00:39:45,600
rather than a radical encounter with the risen Christ

602
00:39:45,600 --> 00:39:47,540
who will transform you from the inside out.

603
00:39:47,540 --> 00:39:49,740
I'm not saying that obedience to Jesus doesn't matter,

604
00:39:49,740 --> 00:39:52,580
but I'm saying when we primarily tell the outsider

605
00:39:52,580 --> 00:39:54,360
that what this is all about is obedience

606
00:39:54,360 --> 00:39:55,820
to a strict code of law,

607
00:39:55,820 --> 00:39:59,060
then they never get past the barrier

608
00:39:59,060 --> 00:40:00,660
to see how beautiful Jesus is.

609
00:40:00,660 --> 00:40:04,200
They never get to have an experience with a risen Jesus

610
00:40:04,200 --> 00:40:06,300
that transforms them from the inside out.

611
00:40:06,300 --> 00:40:11,120
So as insiders, we have to lay down our subcultures

612
00:40:11,120 --> 00:40:15,080
and ideas where we're trying to make people conform to our image.

613
00:40:15,080 --> 00:40:17,120
We have to lay down this idea that Christianity

614
00:40:17,120 --> 00:40:18,800
is just all about following rules.

615
00:40:18,800 --> 00:40:22,520
And we have to invite people to see the risen Jesus.

616
00:40:25,120 --> 00:40:30,080
This is the type of church that can have these two tensions grow together,

617
00:40:30,080 --> 00:40:31,460
that we can grow numerically.

618
00:40:31,460 --> 00:40:33,440
We can reach more people who are outside the church

619
00:40:33,440 --> 00:40:34,680
for the kingdom of God,

620
00:40:34,680 --> 00:40:38,500
and those inside the church can grow together spiritually maturing.

621
00:40:38,500 --> 00:40:41,200
Now, the irony of this message to this point

622
00:40:41,200 --> 00:40:44,020
is that insider and outsider is probably not the proper language.

623
00:40:44,020 --> 00:40:47,960
Because there are people outside the walls of our church

624
00:40:47,960 --> 00:40:50,160
that have embraced the kingdom of God

625
00:40:50,160 --> 00:40:51,920
that look more like Jesus than some of us

626
00:40:51,920 --> 00:40:53,420
that would consider ourselves insiders.

627
00:40:53,420 --> 00:40:57,780
And there are those vice versa.

628
00:40:57,780 --> 00:41:00,680
So the reality is that insider-outsider language

629
00:41:00,680 --> 00:41:01,700
really isn't appropriate.

630
00:41:01,700 --> 00:41:03,760
That the only insiders there are

631
00:41:03,760 --> 00:41:05,320
are those who are willing to surrender

632
00:41:05,320 --> 00:41:06,980
their allegiance fully to King Jesus.

633
00:41:06,980 --> 00:41:10,020
To leave behind their cultural values

634
00:41:10,020 --> 00:41:11,320
and their idols at the door

635
00:41:11,320 --> 00:41:12,600
and to pursue Jesus.

636
00:41:12,600 --> 00:41:14,720
I'd summarize the whole message with this one statement.

637
00:41:14,720 --> 00:41:15,760
Hopefully it makes sense.

638
00:41:15,760 --> 00:41:17,500
The church grows and matures

639
00:41:17,500 --> 00:41:19,560
when people are welcomed as they are

640
00:41:19,560 --> 00:41:22,100
and invited to live by kingdom values

641
00:41:22,100 --> 00:41:23,540
rather than cultural ones.

642
00:41:23,540 --> 00:41:25,120
This is it.

643
00:41:25,120 --> 00:41:27,380
We grow and we mature together

644
00:41:27,380 --> 00:41:29,700
when people who are outside the church

645
00:41:29,700 --> 00:41:31,880
are welcome to come in as they are

646
00:41:31,880 --> 00:41:32,680
with their baggage,

647
00:41:32,680 --> 00:41:33,460
with their questions,

648
00:41:33,460 --> 00:41:34,140
with their doubts,

649
00:41:34,140 --> 00:41:36,840
and when all of us who are both inside and outside

650
00:41:36,840 --> 00:41:39,240
are invited to put down our cultural values

651
00:41:39,240 --> 00:41:41,520
and embrace the values of the kingdom of God.

652
00:41:41,520 --> 00:41:42,640
Imagine a church

653
00:41:42,640 --> 00:41:45,560
that is effective at welcoming the outsider

654
00:41:45,560 --> 00:41:47,960
and challenging the insider

655
00:41:47,960 --> 00:41:49,400
where we grow not just in number

656
00:41:49,400 --> 00:41:50,620
but in spiritual maturity.

657
00:41:50,620 --> 00:41:54,700
Because this is how we embody God's kingdom on earth

658
00:41:54,700 --> 00:41:56,520
as it is in heaven.

659
00:41:56,520 --> 00:41:58,640
Not through just growing a big church

660
00:41:58,640 --> 00:42:00,480
that has cultural influence,

661
00:42:00,480 --> 00:42:03,040
but through growing a mature church

662
00:42:03,040 --> 00:42:05,200
who centers their community on obedience

663
00:42:05,200 --> 00:42:07,440
to the Sermon on the Mount,

664
00:42:07,440 --> 00:42:09,500
the values of the kingdom of God.

665
00:42:11,560 --> 00:42:12,920
As we wrap up,

666
00:42:12,920 --> 00:42:14,800
we're gonna be taking communion together.

667
00:42:14,800 --> 00:42:16,780
I forgot to announce that in my announcements.

668
00:42:16,780 --> 00:42:17,640
I had one announcement.

669
00:42:17,640 --> 00:42:19,160
I actually had two

670
00:42:19,160 --> 00:42:20,980
and I only did one of them.

671
00:42:20,980 --> 00:42:23,200
So if you haven't had time to grab one of these yet,

672
00:42:23,200 --> 00:42:25,540
I'd invite you to do so.

673
00:42:25,540 --> 00:42:28,180
Communion is for those of us

674
00:42:28,180 --> 00:42:31,360
who have embraced allegiance to the kingdom of God.

675
00:42:31,360 --> 00:42:32,020
If you're in here,

676
00:42:32,020 --> 00:42:34,580
you're one of those people who is new to church

677
00:42:34,580 --> 00:42:35,440
or still wrestling,

678
00:42:35,440 --> 00:42:37,280
no pressure to take this.

679
00:42:37,280 --> 00:42:39,040
I'd actually invite you not to take this,

680
00:42:39,660 --> 00:42:43,580
to take time to consider the claims of Jesus.

681
00:42:43,580 --> 00:42:48,700
So as we turn towards communion,

682
00:42:48,700 --> 00:42:50,200
I wanna land the plane.

683
00:42:50,200 --> 00:42:51,820
We've talked a lot about kingdom values

684
00:42:51,820 --> 00:42:53,520
and we're gonna talk more about them next week.

685
00:42:53,520 --> 00:42:54,460
Sorry, Jamie.

686
00:42:54,460 --> 00:42:55,700
I told you I would tell you to come up.

687
00:42:55,700 --> 00:42:56,080
There you go.

688
00:42:56,080 --> 00:42:59,560
We're gonna talk more about them last week,

689
00:42:59,560 --> 00:43:03,100
but there's one that just jumps off the page

690
00:43:03,100 --> 00:43:05,660
of our passage this morning that we skipped over.

691
00:43:05,940 --> 00:43:08,960
We haven't read yet and I want to do so

692
00:43:08,960 --> 00:43:12,440
because I think it transitions us well into communion.

693
00:43:12,440 --> 00:43:14,680
Acts chapter 16, verse one.

694
00:43:14,680 --> 00:43:18,700
Paul came to Derbe and then to Lystra

695
00:43:18,700 --> 00:43:21,180
where a disciple named Timothy lived,

696
00:43:21,180 --> 00:43:23,640
whose mother was Jewish and a believer,

697
00:43:23,640 --> 00:43:25,440
but whose father was Greek.

698
00:43:25,440 --> 00:43:28,840
The believers at Lystra and Iconium spoke well of him.

699
00:43:28,980 --> 00:43:31,220
Paul wanted to take him along on the journey

700
00:43:31,220 --> 00:43:33,980
to grow and mature the churches.

701
00:43:33,980 --> 00:43:38,220
Paul wanted to take him along on the journey.

702
00:43:38,220 --> 00:43:40,160
So he circumcised him

703
00:43:40,160 --> 00:43:44,640
because of the Jews that lived in that area

704
00:43:44,640 --> 00:43:47,700
for they all knew his father was Greek.

705
00:43:49,620 --> 00:43:51,940
So you go back and you read Acts chapter 15

706
00:43:51,940 --> 00:43:55,000
and you will hear a Paul who is vehemently opposed

707
00:43:55,000 --> 00:43:57,460
to asking those who aren't circumcised

708
00:43:57,460 --> 00:43:59,280
to be circumcised for the sake of the kingdom.

709
00:43:59,280 --> 00:44:03,140
And you flip over to chapter 16

710
00:44:03,140 --> 00:44:06,380
and you see Paul asking a young man named Timothy

711
00:44:06,380 --> 00:44:09,420
to get circumcised for the sake of the Jews

712
00:44:09,420 --> 00:44:10,360
living in the area.

713
00:44:10,360 --> 00:44:11,040
What area?

714
00:44:11,040 --> 00:44:12,920
Look at verse one, Lystra.

715
00:44:12,920 --> 00:44:16,380
Guess what happened to Paul a couple of years ago in Lystra?

716
00:44:16,380 --> 00:44:17,040
Anybody know?

717
00:44:18,760 --> 00:44:22,060
He was stoned and left outside the gates for dead.

718
00:44:22,060 --> 00:44:24,760
The Jews in Lystra actually thought they killed Paul.

719
00:44:24,760 --> 00:44:29,120
And Paul says to Timothy,

720
00:44:29,120 --> 00:44:33,860
for the sake of those Jews,

721
00:44:33,860 --> 00:44:37,880
to reach them for the gospel,

722
00:44:37,880 --> 00:44:39,920
to reach them for the kingdom of God,

723
00:44:39,920 --> 00:44:41,360
I'm gonna ask you to do something

724
00:44:41,360 --> 00:44:42,520
that you don't have to do.

725
00:44:42,520 --> 00:44:46,500
There is no requirement from Jesus

726
00:44:46,500 --> 00:44:48,060
that you be circumcised.

727
00:44:48,620 --> 00:44:52,740
But I am gonna ask you to lay down your freedoms,

728
00:44:52,740 --> 00:44:54,580
to lay down your preferences,

729
00:44:54,580 --> 00:44:57,380
to lay down your comfort

730
00:44:57,380 --> 00:45:00,040
for the sake of a group of people

731
00:45:00,040 --> 00:45:02,900
who just thought they murdered me a couple of years ago.

732
00:45:02,900 --> 00:45:06,540
And as we turn our eyes to communion,

733
00:45:06,540 --> 00:45:09,460
I just want us to remind ourselves,

734
00:45:09,460 --> 00:45:11,440
this is the hard message of the gospel,

735
00:45:11,440 --> 00:45:13,960
that this is the primary value of God's kingdom.

736
00:45:14,900 --> 00:45:16,100
Sacrificial enemy love,

737
00:45:16,100 --> 00:45:19,320
laying down your freedom for the sake of others.

738
00:45:20,560 --> 00:45:22,380
This is what we're inviting people into.

739
00:45:22,380 --> 00:45:24,580
This is the foolishness of the world.

740
00:45:24,580 --> 00:45:28,720
But the power of God to bring salvation,

741
00:45:28,720 --> 00:45:30,920
the thing that made the church grow

742
00:45:30,920 --> 00:45:32,520
both in number and spiritual maturity,

743
00:45:32,520 --> 00:45:35,300
wasn't just the message delivered,

744
00:45:35,300 --> 00:45:37,580
but the leader's example who delivered it.

745
00:45:37,580 --> 00:45:41,260
Timothy valued for us,

746
00:45:41,260 --> 00:45:44,280
demonstrated for us what kingdom living looks like.

747
00:45:44,320 --> 00:45:46,560
And it looks like laying down your freedom

748
00:45:46,560 --> 00:45:49,640
and your preferences for the sake of your enemy.

749
00:45:49,640 --> 00:45:52,200
Does that sound like anybody?

750
00:45:55,820 --> 00:46:02,020
The only thing that would make Timothy do that

751
00:46:02,020 --> 00:46:05,020
is if he'd had a radical encounter with the risen Jesus,

752
00:46:05,020 --> 00:46:07,980
who on his behalf,

753
00:46:07,980 --> 00:46:10,280
when Timothy was his enemy,

754
00:46:10,280 --> 00:46:13,020
when Timothy had crucified him

755
00:46:13,020 --> 00:46:14,400
and had turned his back on him

756
00:46:14,400 --> 00:46:15,940
and had rebelled against him,

757
00:46:15,940 --> 00:46:19,560
Jesus willingly went to the cross.

758
00:46:19,560 --> 00:46:21,060
It says he could have called down angels

759
00:46:21,060 --> 00:46:22,980
to avoid the cross and destroy his enemies,

760
00:46:22,980 --> 00:46:24,540
but instead he endured the cross.

761
00:46:24,540 --> 00:46:26,000
And as he endured the cross,

762
00:46:26,000 --> 00:46:26,680
he cried out,

763
00:46:26,680 --> 00:46:28,800
forgive them for they know not what they are doing.

764
00:46:28,800 --> 00:46:32,740
This is the truth the church is built on.

765
00:46:32,740 --> 00:46:34,980
This is the truth you and I

766
00:46:34,980 --> 00:46:36,500
are invited our lives to build on.

767
00:46:36,500 --> 00:46:39,260
This is the truth that Timothy lived out

768
00:46:39,260 --> 00:46:41,920
that ultimately led to an early church

769
00:46:41,920 --> 00:46:44,340
who grew both numerically and in maturity.

770
00:46:44,340 --> 00:46:46,160
This is our invitation.

771
00:46:46,160 --> 00:46:49,600
May we be a group of people

772
00:46:49,600 --> 00:46:52,700
who willingly lay down our lives,

773
00:46:52,700 --> 00:46:54,300
our preferences, our freedoms,

774
00:46:54,540 --> 00:46:57,140
for the sake of those around us.

775
00:46:57,140 --> 00:46:59,540
So this morning,

776
00:46:59,540 --> 00:47:01,760
if you have done that,

777
00:47:01,760 --> 00:47:02,820
if you've made that decision,

778
00:47:02,820 --> 00:47:03,960
and like I said earlier,

779
00:47:03,960 --> 00:47:05,220
when we were singing,

780
00:47:05,220 --> 00:47:08,400
it's a hard message to preach

781
00:47:08,400 --> 00:47:09,660
and it's an even harder one to live.

782
00:47:09,660 --> 00:47:10,840
That's a hard song to sing

783
00:47:10,840 --> 00:47:12,040
and it's an even harder one to live.

784
00:47:12,040 --> 00:47:13,660
You will not do this perfectly

785
00:47:13,660 --> 00:47:15,160
and you have not done this perfectly.

786
00:47:16,360 --> 00:47:17,480
And that's why we,

787
00:47:17,480 --> 00:47:19,100
as often as we can,

788
00:47:19,100 --> 00:47:20,840
look to Christ's body broken for us

789
00:47:20,840 --> 00:47:22,040
and his blood shed for us

790
00:47:22,040 --> 00:47:23,380
because even when we can't,

791
00:47:23,380 --> 00:47:23,900
he can't.

792
00:47:23,900 --> 00:47:27,620
So if you've trusted in Christ,

793
00:47:27,620 --> 00:47:29,120
this morning I invite you

794
00:47:29,120 --> 00:47:31,340
to take his body broken for you.

795
00:47:40,180 --> 00:47:42,840
Drink his blood shed for you.

796
00:47:42,840 --> 00:47:50,720
Father in heaven,

797
00:47:50,720 --> 00:47:51,700
may we be a church,

798
00:47:51,700 --> 00:47:56,300
a Christ-centered community of people

799
00:47:56,300 --> 00:47:59,840
who day by day,

800
00:47:59,840 --> 00:48:00,740
week by week,

801
00:48:00,740 --> 00:48:01,720
month by month,

802
00:48:01,720 --> 00:48:03,180
learn more and more

803
00:48:03,180 --> 00:48:04,100
about the ways in which

804
00:48:04,100 --> 00:48:05,040
we are building our life

805
00:48:05,040 --> 00:48:06,400
on cultural values

806
00:48:06,400 --> 00:48:07,600
rather than kingdom ones.

807
00:48:07,600 --> 00:48:10,740
May the Holy Spirit convict

808
00:48:10,740 --> 00:48:11,340
and challenge

809
00:48:11,340 --> 00:48:12,100
and encourage us

810
00:48:12,100 --> 00:48:12,980
to lay down

811
00:48:12,980 --> 00:48:14,240
the values of this culture

812
00:48:14,240 --> 00:48:16,280
for the values of the kingdom.

813
00:48:16,280 --> 00:48:18,680
May we be a church

814
00:48:18,680 --> 00:48:20,400
that both is successful

815
00:48:20,400 --> 00:48:21,340
at reaching those

816
00:48:21,340 --> 00:48:22,780
who don't yet know you

817
00:48:22,780 --> 00:48:27,540
and also grow in maturity

818
00:48:27,540 --> 00:48:30,600
for your glory,

819
00:48:30,600 --> 00:48:31,260
for our good

820
00:48:31,260 --> 00:48:32,060
and the good of our neighbor.

821
00:48:32,060 --> 00:48:34,800
It's in Jesus' name I pray.

822
00:48:34,800 --> 00:48:35,900
Amen.

823
00:48:36,920 --> 00:48:37,860
If you need prayer,

824
00:48:37,860 --> 00:48:38,920
there should be some volunteers

825
00:48:38,920 --> 00:48:40,080
at the prayer tent.

826
00:48:40,080 --> 00:48:42,660
And other than that,

827
00:48:42,660 --> 00:48:44,220
go in peace, church.

828
00:49:04,220 --> 00:49:08,460
Thank you.
